sections.s2_technical:
Three overloads, each building on the previous:

The simplest form `joining()` concatenates all strings with no separator. This produces "RELIANCETCSHDFC" — useful for building keys or tokens.

```java
String concat = Stream.of("RELIANCE", "TCS", "HDFC")
    .collect(Collectors.joining());
// "RELIANCETCSHDFC"
```

The one-argument form `joining(delimiter)` inserts the delimiter between elements but NOT before the first or after the last. This handles the classic off-by-one problem automatically.

```java
String csv = Stream.of("RELIANCE", "TCS", "HDFC")
    .collect(Collectors.joining(", "));
// "RELIANCE, TCS, HDFC"

// Single element: no delimiter added
String single = Stream.of("RELIANCE")
    .collect(Collectors.joining(", "));
// "RELIANCE"  (not ", RELIANCE" or "RELIANCE, ")

// Empty stream: returns empty string
String empty = Stream.<String>empty()
    .collect(Collectors.joining(", "));
// ""
```

The three-argument form `joining(delimiter, prefix, suffix)` wraps the result.

```java
String json = Stream.of("RELIANCE", "TCS", "HDFC")
    .collect(Collectors.joining("\", \"", "[\"", "\"]"));
// ["RELIANCE", "TCS", "HDFC"]

String report = Stream.of("RELIANCE", "TCS", "HDFC")
    .collect(Collectors.joining(", ", "Symbols: [", "]"));
// "Symbols: [RELIANCE, TCS, HDFC]"

// Empty stream with prefix+suffix: returns prefix+suffix only
String emptyWrapped = Stream.<String>empty()
    .collect(Collectors.joining(", ", "[", "]"));
// "[]"  (prefix + suffix, no delimiter, no elements)
```

Why preferred over manual StringBuilder:

```java
// ❌ MANUAL: error-prone edge cases
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("[");
for (int i = 0; i < symbols.size(); i++) {
    if (i > 0) sb.append(", ");  // off-by-one logic
    sb.append(symbols.get(i));
}
sb.append("]");
// Works, but: 5 lines, index tracking, edge case for empty list

// ✅ JOINING: one line, all edge cases handled
String result = symbols.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.joining(", ", "[", "]"));
// Works for: empty list → "[]", one element → "[RELIANCE]", many → "[RELIANCE, TCS, HDFC]"
```

For parallel streams, joining's combiner merges partial StringJoiner results correctly — manual StringBuilder in parallel would need synchronization.

As a downstream collector inside groupingBy, joining is powerful for creating summary strings per group:

```java
Map<String, String> tradeIdsBySide = trades.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Trade::getSide,
        Collectors.mapping(Trade::getTradeId,
            Collectors.joining(", ", "(", ")"))));
// {BUY: "(TRD-001, TRD-002, TRD-004)", SELL: "(TRD-003, TRD-005)"}
```

